	(text "PG->EN DIVIDER ARITHMETIC (F34) -- check before any BOM cost-pass normalises these values.\nPGOOD on U3 is OPEN-DRAIN (T1-64). Three constraints on two resistors:\n  1. PG released (+3V3_D up): EN_3V3_PLL = 3V3 * R3/(R2+R3) must exceed the LDO's V_IH.\n  2. PG asserted low (fault): EN must sit below V_IL.\n  3. +3V3_D absent / PG high-Z: R3 must dominate so EN defaults LOW -- fail-safe off.\nValues above are PLACEHOLDERS. [VERIFY] the chosen LDO's EN threshold table, then solve.\nDo not substitute standard values without re-checking all three inequalities.

FB DIVIDER TOLERANCE NOTE -- the Err figures in STATE.md are E96-SNAP ONLY. The full
stack is snap + resistor tol (both legs) + reference tol. All rails here have wide
windows and clear comfortably; a future tight-window rail reusing this format does not
inherit that luck.
+1V2_CORE is the highest-impedance divider on the board: 100k/100k, R_thev = 50k, and
TPS62823 FB leakage is 10 nA typ / 50 nA max -> 50k * 50nA = 2.5 mV worst case against a
1.2 V rail with +/-5% window (60 mV). Leakage * R_thev << window, by ~24x. Term shown
because the convention is to make the whole inequality visible, not only the tight ones."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.86 199.39000000000001 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "f4b771e1-8b5b-5b70-b53f-3f0aaabea8d2")
	)
	(text "SCOPE: this pass places active devices, dispositions every pin, and defines the\nsequencing and monitor nets. Inductors, bulk/bypass caps and feedback dividers are the\nnext pass -- FB/SW/CBOOT/SET/ILIM pins carry named nets, not no-connects, so nothing\nis left undispositioned in the meantime (F28)."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 149.86 250.19 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "a768b167-9757-59f2-a96b-686ddce98501")
	)
	(text "+/-13V5_A BLOCK (F39, decreed 2026-08-16). Read this before changing any value here.\n\nWHY THE CASCADE IS FORCED, not preferred:\n  An inverting buck-boost stresses its regulator at VIN + |VOUT| (SNVA856 s4.1). On\n  VBATT_PROT that is >= 26.7 + 15.5 = 42.2 V the instant the TVS conducts at all,\n  against the LM61460-Q1's 42 V ABSOLUTE MAX. True for ANY 12 V-system TVS, because a\n  standoff above 24 V breaks down above 26.5 V. Fed from +15V5_A it sees a fixed 31 V.\n  DO NOT 'simplify' U14 by moving its input to the battery.\n  A plain boost on the positive rail is excluded for the mirror reason: TI states that\n  boost converters pass the input straight through the high-side diode when VOUT <= VIN\n  (SNVSBI7B s9.3.8), which would put a clamped load dump on a 20 V LDO input.\n\nWHY f_sw = 2.2 MHz on BOTH stages -- three independent legs:\n  1. LT3045 PSRR has a NOTCH at 500 kHz-1 MHz and RECOVERS by 2 MHz: at 2 V headroom\n     it reads 77 / 72 / 70 / 84 dB at 100k / 500k / 1M / 2M (Rev. D curve G45).\n     The part is BETTER at 2.2 MHz than at half that. This is why LM5175-Q1 (4-switch\n     buck-boost, nicer magnetics) was rejected -- it tops out at 600 kHz, in the notch.\n  2. LM5156-Q1 dither triangle runs at 0.156 x f_sw = 343 kHz. At a 100 kHz f_sw it\n     would land at 15.6 kHz -- INSIDE the audio band.\n  3. Nothing below the fundamental, so no harmonic falls in the AM band. A 440 kHz\n     choice would put its 2nd and 3rd harmonics squarely in it.\n\nNOISE BUDGET vs the [HARD] 50 uVrms, 20 Hz-20 kHz. The two rails are NOT symmetric:\n  LT3094's PSRR curve is its own (G47), not the LT3045's. At 2 V headroom it reads\n  76 / 66 / 72 / 72 dB -- it PEAKS at 100 kHz, where the LT3045 peaks at 2 MHz, and it\n  DECLINES as headroom grows past ~2 V, which is the opposite behaviour. So:\n     +13V5_A : 0.8 (LDO) + 1.8 (50 mVpp ripple thru 80 dB) + 0.35 -> RSS 2.0 uVrms\n     -13V5_A : 0.8 (LDO) + 4.4 (50 mVpp ripple thru 72 dB) + 0.20 -> RSS 4.5 uVrms\n  Margin 28 dB and 21 dB. The budget is carried by the LDOs' AUDIO-BAND PSRR\n  (LT3045 110 dB / LT3094 >=93 dB at 20 kHz), NOT by f_sw -- which is why V-27's\n  unspecified pulse-skip onset cannot break it. Headroom target 1.9-2.0 V is a PSRR\n  specification, NOT a dropout one: dropout is 260 mV (LT3045) / 235 mV (LT3094).\n\nRSET = 135k ON BOTH RAILS: 13.5 V / 100 uA. 135k IS NOT AN E96 VALUE -- E96 runs\n  133, 137. It is E192, hence 0.1%. The 0.1% buys the VALUE, not the accuracy; ISET's\n  own +/-1% dominates. Normalising this to 'a 1% part' finds no such part exists.\n\nU16 EXPOSED PAD IS **IN**, NOT GND. U15 beside it has EP = GND. Same vendor, adjacent\n  part numbers, sold as a matched +/- pair, same SET architecture -- and DIFFERENT lead\n  counts with OPPOSITE pad nets (LT3045 DD = DFN-10, EP=GND; LT3094 DD = DFN-12, EP=IN).\n  Mirroring the positive channel to lay out the negative one shorts IN to GND.\n\nU14 IS A BUCK WIRED AS AN INVERTER (SNVA856). Its GND pins sit at -15V5_A and its\n  regulated output node is SYSTEM GROUND. Its input caps therefore span the full 31 V,\n  not 15.5 V -- 50 V parts, and the DC-bias derating trap applies with force here.\n  BIAS -> -15V5_A is the datasheet's instruction for outputs above 12 V, not a shortcut."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 560.07 560.07 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "ec609684-e105-5a8e-880f-a0cfd9b8ffd0")
	)
	(text "F40 -- CORRECTION APPLIED THIS PASS, recorded because the value looked plausible.\nEvery LT3045 on this sheet carried R_ILIM = 20k. The LT3045 scale factor is\n150mA*kOhm / R_ILIM, and the datasheet gives the worked example outright: '1k programs\n150 mA'. So 20k programmed a 7.5 mA CURRENT LIMIT on rails budgeted 15-200 mA --\nincluding +3V6_BT feeding a radio at ~100 mA. All five would have current-limited.\nILIM now ties to GND, which is the datasheet's own disposition when the programmable\nlimit is unused, and leaves the internal limit governing. Deliberately NOT programmed:\nV-17 (AKM AVDD currents) is still open, and a limit set against an unknown load is the\nsame mistake with a better-looking number.\n\nSTILL PROVISIONAL ON THIS SHEET -- inductor values. The 10uH figures are placeholders\nfrom the first pass, not derivations; at 2.2 MHz a 5 V/6 A buck wants ~1-2.2 uH. They\nare marked PROVISIONAL and belong to the queued saturation-current pass, which must set\nthem from ripple AND from Isat at low line -- not from RMS heating."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 560.07 699.77 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "a155c2c0-2f1a-56e3-a056-604e5501836d")
	)
	(text "HEADROOM ON +/-15V5_A IS A JOINT OPTIMUM SERVING TWO CURVES WITH OPPOSITE SLOPES.\nDo not re-tune the intermediates as if this were a free parameter.\n  LT3045  (+13V5_A): PSRR COLLAPSES below ~1 V headroom -- 84 dB at 2 V, 62 dB at 1 V,\n                     off a cliff below that. It wants MORE headroom.\n  LT3094  (-13V5_A): PSRR PLATEAUS at ~1.5 V and then DECLINES as headroom grows --\n                     73 dB at 1.5 V, 72 dB at 2.0 V, 70.5 at 2.5 V, 68 at 4 V.\n                     It wants LESS headroom.\n~1.9-2.0 V is where both are near their best. MOVING IT HELPS ONE REGULATOR AND HURTS\nTHE OTHER, in opposite directions, which is not a trade anyone re-derives by accident.\nRaising it also costs dissipation on both (2 V x 250 mA = 500 mW each). Sources:\nLT3045 Rev. D curve G45, LT3094 Rev. B curve G47 -- read independently, not inherited."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 560.07 759.46 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "f18add56-1a59-5f35-818c-645545566a99")
	)
	(text "VBATT_PROT FRONT END -- the five claims, written out so they can be checked, not re-derived.\n\n1. FUSE. F1 = 5 A, BOARD-MOUNTED. Board draw ~20 W; at 9 V crank that is 2.2 A, so 5 A\n   carries >2x margin without nuisance-blowing on inrush. ADDITIONAL REQUIREMENT ON THE\n   INSTALL, and it belongs in the manual, not in someone's head: upstream harness fuse\n   <= 15 A, for selectivity -- ours must clear first. An assumed fuse is an unstated\n   requirement wearing someone else's part.\n\n2. TVS CAPABILITY, STATED BY THE TABLE -- NOT 'no load dump reaches this input'.\n   D2 = SM8S24A-class, DO-218AB, 6600 W (10/1000 us), AEC-Q101, molded heatsink.\n   Vwm 24 V, Vbr 26.7/28.1/29.5 V, Vc 38.9 V AT Ippm = 170 A, aT = 0.087 %/degC.\n   Dynamic resistance rd = (38.9 - 28.1)/170 = 0.0635 ohm -- USE THIS, not the 38.9 V\n   headline, which is the clamp at 170 A and never occurs here.\n   ISO 7637-2 pulse 5 for 14 V powertrains is Us = 65 to 87 V, Ri = 0.5 to 4 ohm,\n   td = 400 ms (Vishay 88490 Table 1). The 35 V figure is the SUPPRESSED level only.\n   Capability, against P(400 ms) from Fig. 6's R_thJM = 0.30 degC/W at 0.4 s:\n       allowed = (175 - T_mount)/0.30  ->  500 W from 25 degC, 300 W from 85 degC\n     suppressed 5b, Us* 35 V, Ri 2 ohm :  3.3 A, Vc 28.3 V,   95 W  -> PASSES, 3-5x\n     unsuppressed  Us 79 V, Ri 4 ohm   : 12.5 A, Vc 28.9 V,  362 W  -> passes cold,\n                                                                       MARGINAL hot\n     unsuppressed  Us 79 V, Ri 2 ohm   : 24.7 A, Vc 29.7 V,  732 W  -> EXCEEDED\n     unsuppressed  Us 87 V, Ri 0.5 ohm :  105 A, Vc 34.7 V, 3631 W  -> EXCEEDED 7-12x\n   THE CAPABILITY STATEMENT IS THEREFORE: this board survives SUPPRESSED load dump\n   (centrally-clamped alternator) across the full Ri range with 3-5x margin, and\n   UNSUPPRESSED pulse 5 only at the high-Ri end. It is specified for vehicles with\n   central load-dump suppression. That is a bounded claim backed by the table above,\n   not an assumption -- and it belongs in the acceptance criteria, not in a comment.\n   FALLOUT WORTH KNOWING: the clamp never threatens the bucks. Worst realistic case is\n   Vc = 34.2 V (48 A at Tj = 150 degC, Vbr scaled by aT) against the LM61460's 42 V\n   absmax -- 7.8 V of margin. Reading the headline 38.9 V and adding tempco would have\n   said 44 V and condemned the design; the headline is at a current we never reach.\n\n3. REVERSE STRATEGY -- SERIES FET, AND REVERSE BATTERY IS A FUSE-CLEARING EVENT.\n   NOT seamless, and the reason is thermal, not preference. D2 must sit AHEAD of Q2:\n     - D2 ahead of Q2: clamp current returns to GND without passing through Q2.\n     - D2 behind Q2 (which WOULD give seamless reverse, since Q2 would block and leave\n       D2 unbiased): the entire clamp current then flows THROUGH Q2. At Us 79 V /\n       Ri 1 ohm that is 48 A for 400 ms; at a hot R_DS(on) of ~51 mohm that is 117 W\n       into a D2PAK, ~176 degC rise. Q2 IS DESTROYED. The seamless property costs the\n       FET, so it is not bought.\n   Consequence, stated so nobody re-litigates it: on reverse battery D2 forward-conducts\n   (Vf 1.8 V max at 100 A) and F1 clears. Q2 still earns its place -- it blocks reverse\n   for everything downstream during the fuse-clearing interval and for any sustained\n   reverse below the fuse's clearing current. D2 survives the event: Ifsm = 700 A/8.3 ms.\n   Q2 stress, both polarities:\n     V_DS reverse  = battery reversed, worst case 24 V jump start reversed -> 24 V\n     V_DS forward  = ~0, Q2 is ON; D2 holds the node upstream of it\n     -> 60 V part, >2x on the governing case.\n   GATE is the fragile pin, not the channel. Source can reach the 38.9 V clamp while the\n   gate is pulled to GND, i.e. V_GS = -38.9 V against a +/-20 V rating -> GATE DESTROYED.\n   D3 (15 V) clamps V_GS; R (100k) limits the zener current: (38.9-15)/100k = 239 uA.\n   D3 IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IS NOT A 'PROTECTION EXTRA' -- without it the FET dies on the\n   first transient it was fitted to survive.\n\n4. LC DAMPING vs THE CONVERTERS' NEGATIVE INCREMENTAL RESISTANCE (Middlebrook).\n   Three bucks and a SEPIC are CONSTANT-POWER loads: draw rises as the rail falls, which\n   is a negative incremental resistance and will happily ring an undamped input filter.\n     Load ~20 W. At 9 V crank:  R_neg = -V^2/P = -81/20 = -4.05 ohm  <-- the limit\n     L = 4.7 uH, C = 22 uF nominal -> ~10 uF EFFECTIVE at 14 V (DC-bias derating; use the\n       derated figure here, the nominal one is not the capacitor you have)\n     Z0 = sqrt(L/C) = sqrt(4.7u/10u) = 0.686 ohm ; f0 = 23.2 kHz\n   UNDAMPED, with ~10 mohm ESR:  Q = Z0/ESR = 68  ->  |Zout|peak = Q*Z0 = 47 ohm\n       47 ohm  >>  4.05 ohm   ->  CRITERION VIOLATED BY ~21 dB. This oscillates.\n   DAMPED, Rd = 0.68 ohm (= Z0) with Cd = 47 uF (>= 4x C, blocks DC so Rd burns nothing):\n       Q ~ 1  ->  |Zout|peak ~ 0.69 ohm  <<  4.05 ohm   ->  5.9x, 15.4 dB margin  OK\n   Rd AND Cd ARE A PAIR. Deleting Rd as 'a resistor doing nothing' restores the 47 ohm\n   peak. Replacing Cd with a plain bulk cap puts Rd across DC and burns it.\n   The alternator-whine filter and the stability network are THE SAME TWO COMPONENTS.\n\n5. CLAMP -> SWITCH-STRESS CHAIN, recorded as a consequence-check so a TVS change re-fires it\n   (M7). The SEPIC's FET rating was derived FROM this clamp, not chosen independently:\n       V_clamp(TVS) 38.9 V  +  V_out(SEPIC) 15.5 V  =  54.4 V switch stress  -> 80 V FET\n   IF THE TVS PN CHANGES, THIS NUMBER CHANGES AND Q1's RATING MUST BE RE-CHECKED.\n   FIRED ONCE ALREADY, on the SMBJ24A -> SM8S24A change, and it CONFIRMED rather than\n   propagated: the SM8S24A is the SMBJ24A's voltage triple, so 38.9 V carries over and\n   Q1 sits at 68 % of its 80 V rating, unchanged. Using the realistic clamp (34.2 V hot)\n   rather than the 170 A headline it is 49.7 V, i.e. 62 %.\n\n6. PN IS **SM8S24AHM3**, NOT SM8S24A. The SM8S10A-thru-43A datasheet carries a\n   NOT-FOR-NEW-DESIGNS banner naming SM8S10AHM3 thru SM8S85AHM3 as the successors.\n   Every electrical figure on this sheet is read from the NRND document and must be\n   re-verified against the AHM3 datasheet, which is NOT YET HELD. Suffix decode from the\n   ordering table: A = +/-5 % and unidirectional, H = AEC-Q101 (absent = industry grade).\n   THE H IS LOAD-BEARING -- this part faces the harness, so S7.0 requires the Q-grade,\n   and dropping the suffix silently buys an unqualified part. Same shape as F37.\n   V-28 CLOSED against Vishay doc 98647 rev. 14-Oct-2025 -- the HM3 successor datasheet.\n   SM8S24AHM3 is IDENTICAL to the NRND part on every figure this sheet uses: Vwm 24.0 V,\n   Vbr 26.7/28.1/29.5 V, Ippm 170 A, Vc 38.9 V, aT 0.087 %/degC. Every number in s2 and\n   s5 stands as read. The pre-fired alternative endpoint (43 V at 153 A, from a\n   distributor) is NOT REPRODUCIBLE from the table: 43 V is the STANDOFF of SM8S43AHM3,\n   i.e. a mis-mapped field. Distributor parametrics are transcription, not specification.\n   PACKAGE CHANGED DO-218AB -> DO-218AC, BUT THE LAND PATTERN DID NOT: 15.30 x 10.70 mm,\n   main pad 9.80 x 10.70, lead pad 2.15 x 2.60, gap 3.35 -- the same drawing. AC adds a\n   0.10 mm nominal standoff. No footprint rework; s7's numbers stand.\n   6600 W vs 5200 W IS SETTLED: both appear in the SAME unidirectional part's ratings\n   table, as the 10/1000 us and 10/10 000 us waveforms. It was never a uni-vs-bi split.\n   A-vs-CA is now an IMPROVEMENT, NOT A BLOCKER. The uni part is fully evidenced and\n   passes. A bidirectional SM8S..CA would additionally give seamless reverse and retire\n   the F43 polarity hazard, but it is a SEPARATE document that is not held, so its\n   capability is unpriced. Fitting the uni now costs nothing later -- same land pattern,\n   so that swap is a VALUE change, not a respin.\n   s3's THERMAL elimination of D2-behind-Q2 is polarity-independent and stays dead.\n\n7. FOOTPRINT: DO-218AB. Land pattern 15.30 x 10.70 mm overall (main pad 9.80 x 10.70,\n   lead pad 2.15 x 2.60, gap 3.35), body 13.3-13.7 x 8.3-8.7 mm, height 5.0 mm max.\n   IT DOES NOT STUFF ON AN SMC LAND. The heatsink tab IS THE ANODE, i.e. ground -- so it\n   pours straight into the ground plane, which is also what makes the 400 ms numbers\n   above real: R_thJM = 0.35 degC/W is junction-to-MOUNT, and it is only as good as the\n   copper under the tab. This pad is a Block E thermal input, not just an outline."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 39.37 820.42 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "badac2fb-d3cd-50bb-a602-f537ec7bc1ce")
	)
	(text "MAGNETICS AND CERAMICS -- the sizing pass. Values here are DERIVED and SNAPPED, not typed.

INDUCTORS. L = Vout(Vin-Vout)/(Vin*fsw*dI), sized at MAX Vin because that is where ripple
is worst, then SNAPPED TO E12 and the ripple RE-DERIVED at the snapped value -- a 2.2 uH
part does not give the ripple a 1.98 uH calculation asked for. Emitting the raw number
would be the 135k-resistor defect in a different component class.
  Isat is quoted at 1.6x the peak OPERATING current (Iout + dI/2), NOT at the converter's
  current limit. The gap is deliberate -- BUT IT IS ONLY SAFE UNDER A CONSTRAINT, AND THE
  CONSTRAINT IS A BOM PROPERTY, NOT A NOTE:

  *** THE BUCK AND IBB INDUCTORS MUST BE SOFT-SATURATING -- COMPOSITE / POWDER CORE. ***
  *** HARD-SATURATING FERRITE IS PROHIBITED ON THESE FIVE LINES. ***
  The fallback for an over-current fault is cycle-by-cycle limiting, and a HARD-saturating
  core is precisely what defeats it: at saturation the inductance collapses, di/dt spikes,
  and the current overshoots INSIDE the comparator-to-gate latency -- so the limiter trips
  AFTER the excursion it exists to prevent. A soft-saturating core rolls off gradually,
  di/dt stays bounded, and limiting keeps working through the fault. Same inductance, same
  footprint, same Isat headline on the BOM -- opposite behaviour in the ONLY case the
  headroom was traded away for.
  So this is not 'a bigger part would be safer'. With the right core the fault degrades
  gracefully and the area is genuinely free; with the wrong one the Isat number is a bet
  on interrupt latency. Applies to L(+5V_D), L(+3V3_D), L(+6V2_A), L(+1V2_CORE), L(IBB).
  The SEPIC coupled part is sized to 4 A against a 4 A sense limit and does not rely on it.
  STILL the line to revisit if a rail ever runs hot.
  +6V2_A's 0.25 A load is an ASSUMPTION pending V-17, and its inductor inherits that tag.

CERAMICS -- USE EFFECTIVE CAPACITANCE AT THE WORKING VOLTAGE, NOT THE PRINTED VALUE.
X5R/X7R lose most of their capacitance under DC bias; a 22 uF 25 V X7R 1210 at 15 V is
commonly 8-10 uF. Rule applied on this sheet: RATED VOLTAGE >= 2x WORKING VOLTAGE, and
where a number is load-bearing the EFFECTIVE value is what the calculation uses.
  Two places it is already load-bearing:
  1. The VBATT_PROT filter: NOTE6 s4's Middlebrook margin uses C = 10 uF EFFECTIVE from a
     22 uF part. Substituting a 22 uF 25 V part and believing the label moves Z0 and Q.
  2. U14's input caps span the FULL 31 V (Vin to IC ground). A 50 V part at 31 V is deep
     into its derating curve -- this is the worst-biased ceramic on the board.
  DO NOT substitute a physically smaller case for the same printed value: derating gets
  worse as the case shrinks, so an 0805 22 uF and a 1210 22 uF are different components
  for this purpose even though the BOM line looks identical."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 560.07 900.4300000000001 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "d8f36cfc-a118-571f-b47c-d26c521c28c9")
	)
	(text "+5V_A MUST STAY LINEARLY REGULATED -- AND THE REASON LIVES ON THE DAC SHEET, WHICH IS
WHY IT IS WRITTEN HERE, WHERE THE PROPOSAL TO CHANGE IT WILL ARRIVE (M15).

  U6 (LT3045) supplies +5V_A, which is AVDD/VREF for BOTH AK4458s. AKM p.50, verbatim:
    'This FIR filter attenuates out of band noise and may reduce degradation of the
     analog characteristics caused by a switching regulator, etc. BEST PERFORMANCE
     SHOULD BE ACHIEVED BY CLEAN LINEAR REGULATED AVDD/VREF POWER SUPPLIES AND THE
     DEFAULT SETTING OF THE FIR2-0 BITS. THESE CONDITIONS ARE INCLUDED IN THE ANALOG
     CHARACTERISTICS SPECS.'

  SO THE DACs' 115 dB IS SPECIFIED UNDER TWO CONDITIONS AT ONCE: a linear AVDD/VREF
  AND FIR2-0 = 000 (its default, 0CH). We satisfy both -- but only the FIRST is
  visible on this sheet, and the second is a register nobody is looking at.

  THE HAZARD IS AN ENTIRELY ORDINARY COST SUGGESTION: 'replace U6 with a buck'.
  That is cheaper and more efficient, and it INVALIDATES THE CONDITION UNDER WHICH
  THE 115 dB WE BUDGET AGAINST IS SPECIFIED -- and it turns FIR2-0 from a defaulted
  register into a live decision on a different sheet. AKM's non-default FIR settings
  exist precisely to buy performance back WHEN THE RAIL IS A SWITCHER.

  IF U6 EVER BECOMES A SWITCHER: FIR2-0 must be re-evaluated against Table 20 and the
  S12 acceptance numbers re-derived. DO NOT TREAT THAT AS AN EFFICIENCY EDIT.
  (Adversary 026. Same shape as the F52 charge-pump note and the CLKOUT note -- the
   number is written where the PROPOSAL lands, not where the part lives.)
"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 39.37 1009.65 0)
		(effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top))
		(uuid "608478f1-7de5-51fb-a15e-2f9ca3f33f2f")
	)
	(embedded_fonts no)
)
